@startuml
title How a numeral elects its carrier — and the coupling defect the current rule removes

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.2
skinparam activity {
  BorderColor #1F2937
  FontColor #111827
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #F9FAFB
  BorderColor #9CA3AF
}

start

:**numeral TEXT** arrives at the lexer,
e.g. `0`, `0u32`, `3000000000`, `3r/4r`; <<#DCFCE7>>

:the lex DAG offers one edge per literal
`pattern:` whose regex matches at this span
(maximal munch — ties STAY in the DAG); <<#DCFCE7>>

partition "acceptor — one `eval` per category, and nothing else decides" {
  if (**UInt32** `eval` accepts?
`IntSuffix::from_text(text) == U32`) then (yes)
    :carrier = **UInt32**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  elseif (**Int** `eval` accepts?
`parse_int_lit(text, Some(I32))`) then (yes)
    :carrier = **Int**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  elseif (**BigInt** `eval` accepts?
trailing `n`, OR unsuffixed and
outside the <latex>i32</latex> range) then (yes)
    :carrier = **BigInt**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  elseif (**BigRat** `eval` accepts?
`parse_rational_lit` — needs an `r`) then (yes)
    :carrier = **BigRat**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  elseif (**Fixed** `eval` accepts?
`parse_fixed_lit` — needs `p<scale>`) then (yes)
    :carrier = **Fixed**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  elseif (**Float** `eval` accepts?
`parse_float_lit` — needs `.` or `e`) then (yes)
    :carrier = **Float**; <<#DBEAFE>>
  else (none accept)
    :**no literal carrier** — the span is not a
numeral of this language. `5000000000u32`
fails closed: it carries the declared tail
but overflows the declared width; <<#FEE2E2>>
    stop
  endif
}

:**I1 — the carrier is now fixed.**
It is a function of the TEXT alone: no context,
no parentheses, no neighbouring factor; <<#FEF3C7>>

:a cross-category promotion may still WRAP the
literal (`IntToBigRat(NumLit 7)`) so it reaches the
surrounding operator's category — but wrapping never
changes which carrier holds the value; <<#EDE9FE>>

:the whole-derivation elector
`__mettail_wpda_select_min_weight_realizing`
takes the argmin over `LexicographicWeight`; <<#EDE9FE>>

:**I3 — the election may move freely, but no reading
it can choose RE-SPELLS a token.** Readings may differ
in inert `( … )` grouping — they may not differ in the
spelling of any numeral; <<#FEF3C7>>

stop

note right
  **THE DEFECT THIS RULE REPLACED** (closed 2026-07-27).

  `UInt32`'s `eval` was `parse_int_lit(text, None)` — a
  UNIVERSAL ACCEPTOR — while its `pattern` demanded a
  `u32` tail and its `Display` always wrote one:

  <latex>\mathtt{UInt32{::}parse}(\text{"7"}) = \mathtt{NumLit}(7)</latex>
  <latex>\mathrm{display}(\mathtt{NumLit}(7)) = \text{"7u32"}</latex>

  A term whose display is NOT the surface it was parsed
  from then sat in the forest, so a GLOBAL argmin could
  pick it — and appending a third factor changed which
  of four readings won:

    `(0 + 0) * 0u32`
       ⇒ `IntToBigRat(AddInt 0 0)`      surface stable
    `(0 + 0) * 0u32 * (0p0 bitand 0p0)`
       ⇒ `UInt32ToBigRat(AddUInt32 0 0)`
       ⇒ `(0u32 + 0u32) * 0u32 * …`     surface MOVED

  The root was the ACCEPTOR, not the elector: an `eval`
  strictly wider than its own declared `pattern`.
end note

@enduml
